1. Time Complexity
Time complexity describes how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size (n) increases.
It does not measure the actual time in seconds. Instead, it measures the number of basic operations an algorithm performs.
Example: Constant Time – O(1)
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]
print(arr[2])

2. Space Complexity
Space complexity describes how much extra memory an algorithm uses as the input size grows.
It includes variables, temporary arrays, recursion stack, and other additional storage.
Example: Constant Space – O(1)
def sum_numbers(a, b):
    return a + b

Difference:
Time Complexity = How long the algorithm takes to run as the input grows.
Space Complexity = How much additional memory the algorithm uses as the input grows.

Example: Linear Space – O(n)
arr = [1, 2, 3, 4]
copy = []
for num in arr:
    copy.append(num)
